Parallel file loading and initialization
========================================

Run ./maxima-local --no-init --batch-string='run_testsuite(tests=["rtest_parallel_lifecycle"]);'
The existing registered lifecycle file gains 428 cases, for 495 in total.
The two parallel-load-fixture files are initialization inputs, not registered
input/expected regression files. The Lisp helper installs their observer.
Run these checks in an otherwise idle process.

A parallel body may not load or initialize files. LOAD, LOADFILE, BATCHLOAD,
BATCH/DEMO, core autoload factories, stock and custom autoload hooks, and
translated-call dispatch are checked before initialization. The same rule
applies to caller, worker, nested scopes and the one-thread fallback.
ENSURE-SERIAL-EXECUTION retains its implementation and is defined earlier
in SUPRV1, before the core loader entry points which now call it.

Twenty-one operation variants have real serial initialization controls.
Sixteen file-based variants also use provably absent paths: refusal must
precede path lookup/opening, not merely the execution of a file body.
Each fixture increments a separately locked count and installs an exact
square function under a fresh symbol. Guarded calls must report the precise
translated Maxima error, leave file/stream initialization untouched, consume
neither input channel and preserve the autoload registry. Each refused first
use must then succeed when retried serially after the runners finish. Serial
preloading then parallel calls must return exact squares for negative, zero and positive
inputs without another initialization. Undefined symbolic functions must
remain symbolic. Worker mode falls back on threadless runtimes. Independent
body counts and actual runtime thread identities verify execution and the requested site;
public results must contain all eight scheduled observations.

DEMO needs its own input: BATCH_ANSWERS_FROM_FILE is disabled for that probe,
MAXIMA-RUN-STRING is locally NIL to exercise actual demo mode, and TERMINAL-IO
uses the observer's private QUERY-IO stream on Lisps which read that channel.
Plain batch probes also bind MAXIMA-RUN-STRING locally so they cannot change
the enclosing batch-string driver. These are fixture bindings, not changes
to ordinary serial loading behavior.

The observer restores its global test state and the prior autoload registry
and removes the fresh function/property definitions after runners have joined.
This setup is intentionally outside the parallel body, including for nested
checks. Serial readiness controls establish that a refusal cannot pass merely
because a fixture or hook did nothing.

Why a registry lock is insufficient
----------------------------------
A synchronized first-use probe under SBCL, CCL and ECL returns an unevaluated
call while another worker is loading its definition. Publishing the function
before finishing initialization instead produces 25 in one worker and 50 in
another, with 50 after loading completes. The second caller already sees a
function and bypasses autoload, so locking only the registry cannot protect
it. The reproductions use temporary source files, no compiled-file damage.
See https://github.com/gunterkoenigsmann/maxima-multithreading/issues/41

This guard does not establish that every loaded package is thread-safe, and
arbitrary raw Common Lisp operations are outside its Maxima API contract.
Load packages and complete their initialization before starting independent
parallel computations. Existing mathematical algorithms and expected answers
are unchanged.
